    {
      "id": "q_triads-ch4-checkpoint.rootless-dominant",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "text": "In the key of C, "
        },
        {
          "text": "B diminished",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is "
        },
        {
          "text": "B",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — which is "
        },
        {
          "text": "G7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " with its root taken away, so it carries the same leaning notes. Why is it still not a drop-in replacement for "
        },
        {
          "text": "G7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " at the end of a phrase?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "It has none of the bass leap that makes a dominant land, so it works as a connecting chord mid-phrase rather than as a full stop."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "It has lost the note that pulls to "
            },
            {
              "text": "C",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": ", so nothing leans anywhere."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Three notes is a fragment; a chord needs four to finish a phrase."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "It belongs to a different key — "
            },
            {
              "text": "B diminished",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " has a note outside C major."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        {
          "text": "The tension is all still there: "
        },
        {
          "text": "B",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " a semitone under "
        },
        {
          "text": "C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " a semitone above "
        },
        {
          "text": "E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". What is missing is the root underneath it, so the chord has nothing to step down from. It is not that nothing leans — "
        },
        {
          "text": "B",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is exactly the note that leans. Calling three notes a fragment contradicts the first thing this pathway said: three notes is a whole chord. And it is not out of key: "
        },
        {
          "text": "B",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " are all in C major; that is why it is the one diminished triad the key has."
        }
      ]
