    {
      "id": "q_triads-ch4-checkpoint.the-grip-that-waits",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "text": "C minor in root position on strings 3-4-5 is "
        },
        {
          "text": "x 3 1 0 x x",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Dropping the fifth a fret should make it diminished — but the diminished grip on that set sits at "
        },
        {
          "text": "x 15 13 11 x x",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " instead, on the last fret of the neck. Why?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The "
            },
            {
              "text": "Gb",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " it needs would sit a semitone below the open "
            },
            {
              "text": "G",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " string, and string 3 can't sound it at any fret — so the close voicing waits for the next octave."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A close triad that low would break the app's rule about muddy voicings, so it is pushed up until it passes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The diminished shape repeats every three frets, so fret 15 is simply the copy that got drawn."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Strings 3-4-5 can't hold a diminished triad in root position at all; the grip shown is a different inversion."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        {
          "text": "String 3's lowest note is the open "
        },
        {
          "text": "G3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", and the chord wants "
        },
        {
          "text": "Gb3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — one semitone lower, which no fret can produce. Its next "
        },
        {
          "text": "Gb",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is an octave up at "
        },
        {
          "text": "3·11",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", far too far from fret 1 for a hand, so the whole voicing waits for the next "
        },
        {
          "text": "C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " on string 5. The same obstruction stranded C major's first inversion on strings 4-5-6 at fret 12 and C minor's second inversion on strings 1-2-3 at fret 11. The trap here is the idea that the shape repeats every three frets: the diminished triad does not repeat."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "q_triads-ch4-checkpoint.which-one-repeats",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "text": "One of the two new qualities gives back the same shape every time you rotate it, and the other doesn't. Which, and why?"
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Augmented. Its gaps are "
            },
            {
              "text": "4 + 4 + 4",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": ", so every rotation has the same structure. Diminished's are "
            },
            {
              "text": "3 + 3 + 6",
              "marks": ["code"]
            },
            {
              "text": " — three different shapes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Diminished. Two stacked minor thirds make it symmetrical, so the shape slides in minor thirds. Augmented's inversions are three different shapes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Both. Any triad built from two equal intervals repeats, and both of these are."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Neither. A triad's three inversions are always three different shapes on the neck."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        {
          "text": "Two equal gaps isn't enough — the gap that wraps back round to the root has to match as well. Diminished stacks "
        },
        {
          "text": "3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " but leaves "
        },
        {
          "text": "6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " to get home, so its ladder draws three unevenly spaced bands with a different shape under each. Calling diminished the symmetrical one is the single most common error written about these chords: it describes the four-note diminished seventh, which is "
        },
        {
          "text": "3 + 3 + 3 + 3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and genuinely symmetrical, not the three-note triad."
        }
      ]
    },
