      "answerId": "a",
      "explanation": [
        {
          "text": "Three equal gaps means every rotation is the same chord structure, so the shape has no root of its own — context supplies one. Whichever note the bass plays, or whichever chord the music just came from, is the root the ear hears. One near miss is worth knowing: "
        },
        {
          "text": "Ab/C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is "
        },
        {
          "text": "C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "Eb",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "G#",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — a flat third, not the "
        },
        {
          "text": "E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " this grip actually sounds."
        }
